ly has no vote. She should make a focused argumentative contribution, referencing existing discussion, and possibly bring up a candidate not yet discussed (A, C, D). She should consider the criteria and maybe challenge B or bring up another candidate.\n\nWe have Sarah's own notes: Candidate A anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized. Candidate B is very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, can be grumpy, can be uncooperative. Candidate C can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education, known to be 100% reliable, creates a positive atmosphere with his crew. Candidate D responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as arrogant, relatively weak leadership skills.\n\nWe need to discuss criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nSarah could bring up Candidate C's reliability and crew atmosphere, decision speed, but communication issues. Or Candidate A's anticipation and leadership but criticism issues. Or Candidate D's problem solving and responsibility but arrogance.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate to argue for. Perhaps Sarah could argue that Candidate C, despite communication issues, has 100% reliability, quick correct decisions, positive crew atmosphere, which may outweigh B's crew cooperation issues. She could note that C's difficulty communicating and egocentric nature are concerns but maybe less severe than B's nasty remarks and memory issues. She could propose C as a strong alternative.\n\nAlternatively, she could argue that Candidate A's anticipation and leadership are strong, but