w cooperation. Not willing to further education may affect adaptability.\n\nWe need to decide. Perhaps we can argue that B's stress resilience and technical competence are more critical for long-distance flights, and that his communication issues can be mitigated, while C's lack of adaptability and egocentric behavior may be harder to address. Also C's difficulty communicating ideas is a serious safety risk. So we can stick with B.\n\nThus we will respond: I understand Sarah's points, but I remain with B. Provide evidence: B very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at weather assessment (technical), but we acknowledge communication concerns. However, C's egocentric and unwillingness to further education raise concerns about adaptability and crew cooperation. Also C's difficulty communicating ideas could be more severe than B's tone issues. So B remains best.\n\nThus we produce public message: \"I appreciate Sarah's points, but I remain with B.\" Provide evidence.\n\nMetadata: vote Candidate B.\n\nProceed."}
